// Evolution-stage seeds
evolutionStage
  basic stage, small and cute, simple silhouette, one main feature, rounded proportions
  basic stage, baby-like appearance, oversized head, tiny limbs, expressive eyes
  basic stage, energetic and playful, clean shape language, minimal ornamentation

  middle stage, adolescent appearance, more defined anatomy, additional secondary features
  middle stage, stronger and more confident, evolving armor or markings
  middle stage, longer limbs and larger silhouette, expanded elemental traits
  middle stage, transitional form with visible growths and developing weapons
  middle stage, increased complexity while remaining recognizable from previous stage

  final stage, powerful and imposing, highly recognizable silhouette
  final stage, fully developed elemental traits and signature features
  final stage, legendary-looking presence without being overdesigned
  final stage, battle-ready anatomy, detailed armor and ornamentation
  final stage, elegant and majestic proportions
  final stage, apex predator appearance
  final stage, guardian-like presence with symbolic markings

// Evolution scaling guidance
stageScale
  baby = tiny body, oversized head, simplified features
  basic = compact body, one dominant feature
  middle = larger silhouette, secondary features begin developing
  final = largest silhouette, fully developed design language

// Authentic Gen 3 palette constraints
gbaPaletteRule
  maximum 15 visible colors plus transparent index 0
  shared palette between front and back sprite
  avoid excessive gradients
  clear silhouette readability at 64x64 resolution
  strong hue separation between major body regions
  darkest outline colors use tinted outlines rather than pure black
  limited highlight ramps
  high contrast focal points around face and eyes
  colors compatible with BGR555 quantization
  color ramps survive GBA LCD display limitations
  saturated colors reserved for focal elements
  no anti-aliasing outside sprite boundary
  preserve palette slots for shiny remapping
  avoid near-duplicate colors
  avoid banding in large areas
  use selective outlining
  maintain readability on light and dark backgrounds

// Gen 3 primary palette archetypes
paletteArchetype
  warm reds and cream accents
  cool blues and white highlights
  earthy browns and moss greens
  black and crimson contrast
  gold and royal blue
  emerald green and yellow
  lavender and deep violet
  orange and charcoal
  aqua and teal
  silver and cobalt
  forest green and tan
  pink and magenta
  icy cyan and navy
  volcanic red and obsidian
  sand beige and rust
  jade and bronze
  moonlit blue and silver
  toxic purple and lime
  coral red and cream
  storm gray and electric yellow

// Gen 3 design complexity controls
designComplexity
  very simple, Pikachu-level complexity
  simple, Gen 1 starter complexity
  moderate, Gen 2 evolution complexity
  moderate, Gen 3 regional Pokémon complexity
  advanced, pseudo-legendary complexity
  advanced, final evolution complexity

generationStyle
  designed as an official Generation 3 Pokémon
  designed in Ken Sugimori Generation 3 style
  Ruby Sapphire era creature design
  Emerald version battle sprite design
  FireRed LeafGreen era Pokémon design
  Hoenn-native Pokémon aesthetic
  simple and iconic Game Freak silhouette
  authentic 2002 Nintendo creature design
